   "text": "The results separate two kinds of benefit. The workflow delivers process benefits: a complete assignment guaranteed by construction (single-shot prompting was also valid in every run here, but without such a guarantee), lower and more predictable cost, up to three explicit alternatives (often only one or two distinct), and a complete audit trail from evidence to decision. It does not deliver a quality benefit: its designs are comparable to those of a well-prompted single call, better on some metrics and worse on others, and not more repeatable. This is a narrower claim than “agents produce better architectures,” but it is a useful one. Much of an architect's cost in using LLM output lies in checking it; a workflow that guarantees a well-formed answer, shows its alternatives, and records why it chose one reduces that cost even when the answer itself is no better.",
